=== REASONING ===
Why the selected design preserves ADR-015 and methodology P1-P10:

P1 provider port: the strategy depends only on the dispatcher and
    the typed tool surface; provider clients are reached ONLY via
    the decorator chain (redaction → rate-limit → cost-cap → audit
    → trace), exactly as in slice 0058.

P3 default-off: registry entry has `DefaultOn: false` and PU3 tier.
    Feature toggle defaults to off; backend guard returns 404 in
    off mode; frontend HOC returns null in off mode.

P4 strategy/dispatcher loop: strategy.go exposes a Strategy with a
    SystemPrompt enumerating the curated scope and the propose-only
    contract; the dispatcher loop in F4 + F5 handles user→assistant
    rounds + tool_call/tool_result interleaving without bespoke
    plumbing in the feature code.

P5 decorator chain: handler invokes the dispatcher which in turn
    invokes the canonical decorator chain in the locked order. No
    direct provider client import in this slice's feature code
    (strategy or handler).

P6 backend guard + frontend wrapper: handler is wrapped by
    `g.Wrap("nl-dashboard-composer", ...)` in mountAIRoutes; the
    InnerSection React component is exported via
    `withAiFeature('nl-dashboard-composer', InnerSection)`. Both
    sides converge on the same feature ID; on/off contract holds
    end to end.

P7 per-feature toggle: tested via the AISettings.ai_features map
    (Settings → AI). The off-mode SPA test toggles a fake settings
    fixture and asserts the AI surface DOM is absent; the on-mode
    SPA test does the inverse.

P8 eval goldens: 4 goldens in goldens.yaml; canned mock-provider
    responses for each; eval harness runs cleanly (`go run
    ./cmd/ai-eval --feature nl-dashboard-composer` reports 4/4
    PASS).

P9 feature registry coverage: the registry entry carries Backend,
    Frontend, UITestIDs, JobNames (empty), PushKinds (empty)
    explicitly. `aivet` confirms RouteSet coverage AND that
    web/src/ai/features.ts is in sync with the Go registry (after
    `aigen` regeneration).

P10 baseline coexistence: DashboardComposerAssistant selection is
    `ai_mode != 'off' && ai_features['nl-dashboard-composer']`,
    gated inside withAiFeature; the manual JSON composer
    + curated catalog viewer + Copy-to-clipboard remain the
    canonical baseline for off-mode users. The deterministic
    baseline ALWAYS renders, regardless of toggle state — AI is
    layered alongside.

P11/P12 wired-or-absent: AINLDashboardComposer imports useAiStream
    and POSTs against /ai/power/dashboard/draft; the action button
    has a computed `disabled` expression (`!canDraft` where
    canDraft is `prompt.trim().length > 0 && stream.state ===
    'idle'`); no literal disabled; no "future slice"/"coming
    soon"/"wiring lands"/"would call POST" strings in the shipped
    file. The double-submit guard (no-op while
    `stream.state === 'streaming'` or `'paused-confirm'`) is
    asserted in the on-mode wiring test.

HX (Helix UX) scaffold compliance: the AI surface renders through
    the shared `AIFeatureCard` (NOT a bespoke GlassPanel + Button
    composition). The per-feature verb "Draft dashboard" is passed
    via `buttonLabel`; the card paints "Ask Helix · Draft dashboard"
    as the accessible name and visible label. The HelixMark brand
    glyph + AIThinkingDots are managed by the card. User-visible
    i18n copy says "Helix" (not "AI"). The on-mode wiring test
    locates the CTA via the unanchored regex `/Draft dashboard/i`.

ADR-015 invariants honored end to end (see footer for evidence
per invariant).

Why per-request scope binding via context.Context: keeps the
typed tools (`draft_dashboard_layout`/`validate_dashboard_layout`)
re-usable as pure ports while letting the handler compute the
curated panel-template catalog per request. Mirrors the per-request
scope binding used by `WithGrafanaPanelScope` /
`GrafanaPanelScopeFromContext` in slice 0058 and
`WithNLSqlPlaygroundScope` in slice 0057. Defense-in-depth: even
if the LLM picks a panel_name outside the curated scope, the typed
tool refuses BEFORE any side-effect proposal escapes the dispatcher.

Why a static curated catalog of 6 panel templates: the prompt asks
for a "natural-language dashboard composer"; composing a dashboard
from PRE-CURATED named panels keeps this slice tightly scoped, lets
the LLM focus on layout (not panel internals), and lets the F8
redaction policy stay deny-by-default (no aggregate metadata leaves
the process). Real future slices may swap the static catalog for a
user-defined library of panels saved by nl-grafana-panel — the
`AINLDashboardComposerCatalogSource` port already supports that
substitution.

Why 12-slot ceiling: matches the typical Grafana dashboard density
(rule of thumb: 12 panels above the fold on a 1080p screen with a
24-col grid). The validator refuses >12 to keep proposals
reviewable.

Why no overlapping bounding boxes: a dashboard with overlapping
panels is a UX defect that would never pass review; refusing it at
the typed-tool boundary prevents the LLM from proposing dashboards
the user would immediately reject. Pairwise SAT (separating-axis
test on axis-aligned bounding boxes) is O(n²) on n ≤ 12 = at most
66 pairs — trivial.

Off-mode contract impact:
    backend routes added:        POST /api/v1/ai/power/dashboard/draft
    frontend routes affected:    /power/dashboards (baseline ALWAYS renders;
                                  AI surface conditional)
    UI test IDs to assert absent: ai-feature-nl-dashboard-composer-root
    new background jobs:         none
    new push kinds:              none
    service worker chunks:       ai-nl-dashboard-composer (registered in registry only;
                                  off-mode test asserts that the AI surface is never
                                  mounted, so the chunk is never fetched)
    client storage keys:         ai.dashboardComposer.draft (used by the deterministic
                                  baseline JSON composer Textarea — runs in both modes;
                                  AI side never writes to it)

